class Solution {
    static boolean isEven(int n) {
        // --- 1. MODULO OPERATOR APPROACH ---
        // Implementation: 
        // return (n % 2) == 0;
        //
        // WHAT IT IS: 
        // The modulo operator (%) divides the number 'n' by 2 and returns 
        // the remaining value (remainder).
        //
        // WHY CHOOSE MODULO OVER BITWISE AND:
        // * Readability: It is highly intuitive and easy for any developer to 
        //   read and understand instantly.
        // * Standard Practice: It is the standard mathematical way taught in 
        //   programming, making it ideal for everyday business logic where 
        //   extreme execution speed is not a priority.

        // --- 2. BITWISE AND OPERATOR APPROACH ---
        // Implementation:
        return (n & 1) == 0;
        
        // WHAT IT IS: 
        // The bitwise AND (&) operator compares numbers at the binary level 
        // bit-by-bit. Using '& 1' acts as a binary mask that ignores all 
        // higher even columns and isolates only the very last bit.
        //
        // WHY CHOOSE BITWISE AND OVER MODULO:
        // * Performance: It is drastically faster because it bypasses slow 
        //   CPU division math and executes in a single hardware cycle.
        // * Negative Numbers: It naturally handles negative numbers correctly 
        //   without needing extra code logic.
    }
}
